\chapter{Media clocks, frame layouts, and image or audio providers}
\label{ch:106}
\index{Media clocks, frame layouts, and image or audio providers}

Media kernels cover timebases, frame metadata, media buffers and selected transforms. A timestamp has a unit and origin. A frame often has separate width, height, stride and format, while an audio buffer needs its channel layout and sample format. Kernel discovery can report declarations and the supported native provider profile. The media inventory by itself grants no general checked On1x calls.

\section{Test metadata before payloads}
Convert a timestamp between two declared timebases and choose a test where rounding is visible. For a frame, use a stride larger than its visible row width and prove that consumers respect both fields. For audio, choose samples that expose channel ordering. Check whether native dependencies provide the actual algorithm or only the bounded adapter profile. Determine who releases a returned view before retaining it across operations. Comparing checksums on two frame buffers makes sense only after confirming they use the same format, layout and inclusion of padding.

\section{Calculate the last visible byte}
Construct a borrowed RGB8 frame three pixels wide and two rows high.
There are nine visible bytes per row. Give the frame a stride of twelve
bytes: the first row occupies offsets zero through eight, the second
twelve through twenty. The plane needs at least 21 live bytes, not 18
(which forgets the gap) and not necessarily 24 (which counts padding
after the last row). The plane extent helper computes this as one
stride plus the last row's visible byte count. With the same metadata
and only twenty live bytes it should reject the plane before a copy.

Use disjoint destination storage and initialize its gap bytes to a
distinct marker. A successful copy moves each nine-byte row and copies
the presentation timestamp and timebase. Check the gap separately: a
single contiguous copy of 18 bytes from the start would include padding
and omit visible pixels. A destination view starting one byte into the
source's storage is not an in-place equivalent layout; the frame copy
rejects overlapping extents rather than promising memmove semantics.
Keep ownership outside this exercise: neither borrowed view is an
argument for the frame allocator's release operation.

Now compare the units in the analogous audio calculation. Three stereo
frames contain six interleaved float samples. The finite-sample scan
takes six, whereas the byte extent is six times the size of a float.
Place a nonfinite value in the final channel of the last frame. Passing
three as the scan count would miss it even though the allocation and
all the pointer arithmetic were valid.

